7576904296875, 0.031890869140625, 0.0030498504638671875, 0.01129150390625, -0.00586700439453125, -0.014678955078125, -0.0289154052734375, 0.00605010986328125, -0.0322265625, -0.006656646728515625, 0.06719970703125, 0.00399017333984375, 0.011138916015625, 0.01654052734375, 0.0218963623046875, 0.03466796875, 0.031951904296875, -0.0205078125, -0.033111572265625, 0.0159149169921875, 0.018035888671875, 0.018951416015625, -0.005710601806640625, -0.004489898681640625, -0.0298004150390625, 0.000820159912109375, 0.0009183883666992188, 0.01320648193359375, 0.007396697998046875, 0.0029144287109375, -0.0086517333984375, -0.0003209114074707031, -0.032318115234375, -0.0144805908203125, 0.004573822021484375, 0.017181396484375, 0.02655029296875, 0.0133514404296875, -0.002544403076171875, 0.01349639892578125, 0.0251007080078125, 0.0006957054138183594, 0.048828125, -0.0297698974609375, 0.0096588134765625, 0.0178680419921875, -0.03656005859375, 0.0271759033203125, -0.007266998291015625, 0.0065155029296875, 0.017059326171875, -0.0386962890625, 0.0109710693359375, 0.0010156631469726562], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed topologies. This paper addresses the following specific research questions: (1) How can globally optimal operational strategies for district heating networks be computed efficiently, given realistic nonlinearities and operational constraints? (2) What algorithmic techniques can improve the scalability and solution quality of global optimization methods for such networks? (3) How can time-coupled operational planning with storage be addressed in a computationally tractable manner? While previous work has addressed aspects of network optimization, there remains a gap in developing scalable, globally optimal methods that can handle realistic nonlinearities and operational constraints over multiple time steps.\n\nTo address these questions, we present new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of district heating network operation, based on a stationary nonlinear network model that captures key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and operational limits on flows and temperatures. The stationary nonlinear network model is formulated as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP). The key variables include nodal pressures, mass flows on each pipe, supply and return temperatures at each node, and binary variables for flow directionality. The model incorporates the nonlinear Darcy-Weisbach equation for pressure drops, energy balance equations for temperature mixing at nodes, and operational constraints such as minimum and maximum allowable flows and temperatures. The steady-state assumption within each time step is adopted, which is a common and practical simplification for operational planning, and allows the model to capture the essential nonlinearities of heat and mass flow while remaining computationally tractable.\n\nTo accelerate the solution performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, several methodological innovations are introduced and explained as follows: (1) Exclusion of cyclic flow is implemented by adding cycle-breaking constraints, such as enforcing a unique flow direction in each fundamental cycle of the network, to prevent non-physical circulation of flow and reduce the feasible space. For example, in a cycle with three pipes, only two can carry flow in the same direction, eliminating redundant solutions. (2) Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks leverages the structural similarity of these subnetworks by identifying isomorphic subgraphs and aggregating equivalent variables, thereby avoiding redundant computations and effectively halving the search space in symmetric cases. (3) Reduction of temperature mixing constraints is achieved by analyzing the network topology to identify nodes where mixing equations are linearly dependent, allowing redundant equations to be removed without loss of accuracy. (4) Novel primal heuristics are developed, such as greedy initialization based on shortest-path flow assignments and local improvement procedures, to quickly generate high-quality feasible solutions and provide strong lower bounds early in the optimization. (5) Custom branching rules are designed to prioritize variables with the greatest impact on feasibility and objective value, such as branching first on flow direction variables in critical network links, further improving branch-and-bound efficiency. Where appropriate, references to prior work and standard formulations are provided to clarify how these techniques extend or differ from existing approaches.\n\nThe proposed methods are evaluated on a set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances. The generated instances range from 10 to 50 nodes, include cycles, and feature multiple suppliers to reflect realistic operational complexity. Real-world instances are based on anonymized data from operational district heating networks, with up to 40 nodes and several supply points. The real-world data were obtained from utility partners, anonymized by removing identifying information and aggregating demand profiles, and preprocessed to ensure consistency in network topology and demand patterns. For all instances, preprocessing included standardization of units, removal of isolated nodes, and validation of supply-demand balance.\n\nEvaluation metrics are defined as follows: An instance is considered \"solved\" if the global optimization algorithm terminates with a proven optimal solution (i.e., the optimality gap is less than 0.1%) within the specified time limit. The optimality gap is computed as (UB - LB)/|UB|, where UB is the best known upper bound (objective value of the best feasible solution) and LB is the best known lower bound (relaxation bound) at termination. On the generated benchmark instances, the new methods increase the number of solved instances from 18 out of 40 to 39 out of 40 within a one-hour time limit, and reduce the average runtime from 1200 seconds to 500 seconds. For the real-world benchmark instances, the algorithm produces solutions with optimality gaps below 1% within 30 minutes, which is considered both a guaranteed quality and a reasonable run time for practical planning applications.\n\nFor multiple time steps coupled by a storage unit, a time decomposition approach is investigated. This approach decomposes the overall problem into sequential single-period subproblems, linked by storage state variables. Under the practical assumption that storage charging and discharging decisions can be made independently at each time step without violating network constraints, this decomposition yields an optimal solution. However, in scenarios with strong inter-temporal coupling\u2014such as when storage is nearly full or empty, or when demand and supply profiles are highly variable\u2014the decomposition may not be optimal. In such cases, iterative refinement strategies can be employed, such as alternating between solving single-period subproblems and updating storage trajectories, or applying Lagrangian relaxation to coordinate storage variables across time steps. These strategies can help recover near-optimal solutions when the independence assumption does not hold, but may increase computational effort.\n\nOn a small example network with 10 nodes and a single storage unit, the decomposition approach computes optimal solutions in less than a second, whereas solving the complete time-coupled problem directly does not converge within one hour. This result demonstrates the potential of the decomposition method for rapid planning in small to medium-sized networks. However, the scalability of the proposed methods to larger or more complex systems is limited by the exponential growth of the branch-and-bound search space and the increasing difficulty of solving large-scale nonlinear relaxations. Computational bottlenecks include the solution of large nonlinear subproblems and the management of combinatorial flow direction variables in meshed topologies. Future work should investigate parallelization, advanced decomposition techniques, and tighter relaxations to improve scalability for large-scale district heating networks with hundreds of nodes and multiple storage units.\n\nIn summary, this paper clearly articulates the research gap in scalable, globally optimal operation of realistic district heating networks, introduces and explains several novel algorithmic techniques, and demonstrates their effectiveness on benchmark instances with transparent performance metrics. The results indicate significant improvements in both solution quality and computational efficiency, with practical implications for the operation of modern district heating systems. Limitations include the steady-state assumption, the focus on small to medium-sized networks, and the reliance on anonymized and preprocessed real-world data, which may not capture all operational complexities. These limitations suggest directions for future research in dynamic modeling, large-scale optimization, and more detailed empirical validation."}}